# Site configuration for the nf-core/hlarnaseq HPC module.
#
# Copy to site.env next to this file and edit. This is the ONLY file a deployer
# needs to change:
#
#     cp site.env.example site.env
#
# site.env is optional. Without it the wrappers still work - they just leave
# Nextflow's own defaults in place rather than inventing site paths.
#
# Precedence: a variable already set in the user's environment always wins over
# site.env, and site.env wins over the built-in defaults. So a user can do
#
#     NXF_SINGULARITY_CACHEDIR=/my/own/cache hlarnaseq ...
#     QUEUE=long hlarnaseq-bsub ...
#
# without editing anything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared cache the public Biocontainers/Galaxy-depot images are pulled into
# (STAR, Subread, samtools, HLA-LA, HIBAG, validatefastq). Users need only read
# access; whoever primes it needs write access.
#
# NOTE: this does NOT cover the four locally built images. Those are referenced
# by the modules as ${projectDir}/containers/datatools/datatools.sif and
# ${moduleDir}/*.sif - i.e. by path inside the install tree, not through this
# cache. See README.md § "The four local images".
#HLARNASEQ_SINGULARITY_CACHEDIR=/software/hgi/containers/hlarnaseq

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nextflow profile
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `singularity` enables the container engine; `sanger` comes from nf-core/configs
# and supplies the LSF executor, farm22 queue selection, and the
# --bind /lustre,/nfs,/data,/software mounts. `sanger` does NOT enable
# Singularity by itself, so both are needed and in this order.
#HLARNASEQ_PROFILE=singularity,sanger

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hlarnaseq-bsub head job
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The head job is one core that mostly waits, for as long as the whole run
# takes. `oversubscribed` is the farm22 queue intended for exactly that; use
# `long` or `basement` if it is not available to you.
#HLARNASEQ_QUEUE=oversubscribed

# Memory reservation for the head job, in MB. Keep HLARNASEQ_JAVA_OPTS's -Xmx
# comfortably below this: the JVM heap is not the whole process.
#HLARNASEQ_MEM_MB=8000
#HLARNASEQ_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms1G -Xmx5G
